# branching (Cursor)

Manage feature branches in the paired-branch + cherry-pick-to-staging git
model. Not generic git-flow — verify first (below) before applying any of
this.

## Detect first

Check `git branch -a` for `releases/(sandbox|staging|main)` branches and
`-main`/`-dev` suffixed feature branches, and check CI config for deploy
jobs triggered on push to `releases/*`. If none of this is present, this
model doesn't apply — don't invent it.

## New feature branch

Branch from the trunk (`main`), never from staging or another feature
branch. Name per this repo's convention (`<type>/<slug>-main`). Check
recent commit history on a similar branch for a required commit-message
convention before assuming one — some repos require every commit to be
prefixed with a feature-name tag because commits get cherry-picked later.

## Sync to staging

The staging branch is shared across many features — the two failure modes
to avoid are duplicate cherry-picks and missed commits (the second is what
causes "staging ketinggalan banyak fitur").

1. `git log <slug>-dev..<slug>-main --oneline` — only these commits are new
   since the last sync; never re-pick anything already on `-dev`.
2. If `-dev` doesn't exist, create it from `-main`'s current head, not from
   staging's current state.
3. Cherry-pick the new commits onto `-dev` with `git cherry-pick -x` so the
   origin SHA is recorded — this is what makes the next sync's diff
   reliable.
4. Resolve conflicts commit-by-commit; a conflict usually means another
   feature already touched the same code on staging — understand both
   changes rather than blindly taking one side.
5. Before merging into the shared staging branch, check
   `git log releases/sandbox..<slug>-dev --oneline` to confirm exactly
   what's about to land.
6. Merge or PR into staging per this repo's convention. Never force-push a
   shared release branch.

## Promote to production

Confirm testing sign-off per this repo's process first. Open a PR from
`<slug>-main` (not `-dev`) into the trunk. Check whether merging to `main`
alone triggers deploy, or whether a separate push/merge to a
`releases/main`-style branch is required — don't assume.

## Not this skill's job

- Not for repos using plain GitHub Flow or trunk-based development.
- Not a way to skip required review/testing sign-off.
